# Wireshark Filters Cheatsheet

## Display filters (applied after capture)
| Goal | Filter |
|------|--------|
| One host | `ip.addr == 192.168.56.20` |
| Source or destination only | `ip.src == 192.168.56.10`, `ip.dst == 192.168.56.20` |
| One port | `tcp.port == 80` |
| Protocol | `dns`, `http`, `icmp`, `tls`, `ssh`, `arp` |
| Combine | `ip.addr == 192.168.56.20 && tcp.port == 22` |
| Either | `dns \|\| icmp` |
| Exclude noise | `!arp && !mdns` |
| TCP SYN (start of connection) | `tcp.flags.syn == 1 && tcp.flags.ack == 0` |
| TCP resets (closed ports, dropped connections) | `tcp.flags.reset == 1` |
| Retransmissions (network problems) | `tcp.analysis.retransmission` |
| DNS queries for one name | `dns.qry.name contains "example"` |
| HTTP requests | `http.request` |
| Text inside packets | `frame contains "text"` |

## Capture filters (BPF, set before capturing)
| Goal | Filter |
|------|--------|
| One host | `host 192.168.56.20` |
| One port | `port 53` |
| A network | `net 192.168.56.0/24` |
| Only TCP | `tcp` |
| Not SSH (when capturing over SSH) | `not port 22` |

## Useful actions
| Action | How |
|--------|-----|
| Follow a conversation | Right-click a packet, **Follow > TCP Stream** |
| Statistics | **Statistics > Conversations** and **Protocol Hierarchy** |
| Export objects | **File > Export Objects > HTTP** |
| Save only what I need | **File > Export Specified Packets** |

## Defender patterns to recognise
| Pattern | Suggests |
|---------|----------|
| Many SYN packets to many ports from one source | A port scan |
| Many failed connections or resets | Scanning or blocked traffic |
| Large outbound transfer to an unknown address | Possible data leaving |
| DNS queries for long random-looking names | Possible malware or tunnelling |
| Passwords visible in HTTP, FTP, Telnet streams | Missing encryption: fix it |

Captures can contain private data. Keep `.pcap` files out of Git (they are in `.gitignore`).
